        "e": "<strong>Threshold cycle (Ct) inverse relationship in quantitative real-time PCR (Topic 638):</strong> In real-time quantitative PCR, the threshold cycle ($C_t$ or $C_q$) is defined as the fractional PCR cycle number at which reporter fluorescence surpasses a predefined statistical baseline threshold above background noise during the exponential phase. PCR amplification operates via geometric expansion: under optimal 100% reaction efficiency, each thermal cycle doubles the number of target double-stranded amplicon copies according to the exponential equation $N = N_0 \times 2^n$, where $N_0$ represents initial template molecules and $n$ is cycle number. Consequently, a sample harboring a higher initial starting concentration of target mRNA (and hence higher initial cDNA template copy number) requires fewer rounds of geometric doubling to accumulate the critical number of fluorophores needed to cross the fluorescence threshold. Therefore, $C_t$ is strictly inversely proportional to the logarithm of initial template concentration.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is a Mechanism Misattribution trap; free dNTPs in the reaction buffer do not quench fluorophores, and higher template abundance decreases rather than increases the number of cycles required to reach threshold.<br>&bull; C is a Directional Inversion trap; enzyme saturation does not occur at the early exponential threshold crossing, and abundant templates shift the $C_t$ to the left (lower cycle numbers), not to the right.<br>&bull; D is a Factual Error trap; initial PCR kinetics are strictly exponential without an inverted or extended lag phase for high-abundance templates."
