        "e": "<strong>Phred quality score logarithmic metrics in high-throughput sequencing (Topic 640):</strong> The Phred quality score ($Q$) is a universally standardized logarithmic metric characterizing the probability ($P$) that a specific base call generated during DNA sequencing is incorrect. The relationship is mathematically defined by the logarithmic equation: $Q = -10 \\log_{10}(P)$, which rearranges to $P = 10^{-Q/10}$. For a score of $Q = 30$ ($Q30$): $P = 10^{-30/10} = 10^{-3} = 0.001$, representing a **1 in 1,000 probability** of an incorrect base call. The corresponding base call accuracy is calculated as: $\text{Accuracy} = 1 - P = 1 - 0.001 = 0.999$, or **99.9% accuracy**. In high-throughput NGS pipelines, $Q30$ is widely recognized as the benchmark standard for high-confidence clinical genomic data.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B corresponds to a Phred score of Q20 ($P = 10^{-20/10} = 0.01 = 1/100$, 99% accuracy).<br>&bull; C corresponds to a Phred score of Q40 ($P = 10^{-40/10} = 0.0001 = 1/10,000$, 99.99% accuracy).<br>&bull; D is a Linear Scale Confusion trap; Phred scores are logarithmic ($Q = -10 \\log_{10} P$), not linear percentages."
