        "e": "<strong>Alkaline denaturation in Southern blot hybridization (Topic 641):</strong> In a Southern blot, genomic DNA digested by restriction endonucleases resides in the agarose gel as intact, double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) duplexes held together by Watson-Crick hydrogen bonding between complementary base pairs. Hybridization with a labeled probe relies on base pairing with the target sequence. Because the probe cannot anneal to a target sequence that is already paired within a stable duplex, the DNA fragments must be denatured prior to or during transfer. Soaking the gel in an alkaline solution (e.g., 0.5 M NaOH with 1.5 M NaCl) disrupts interstrand hydrogen bonds, converting dsDNA into **single-stranded DNA (ssDNA)**. Once transferred and immobilized on the positively charged nylon membrane, these single-stranded target sequences are accessible to anneal with the single-stranded radiolabeled or fluorescent probe.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B describes depurination/acid nicking or non-specific hydrolysis; extensive backbone destruction would destroy the target sequence.<br>&bull; C is a Physics Inversion trap; DNA is negatively charged (anions) due to its phosphate backbone, and alkali does not confer a positive charge.<br>&bull; D is a Biochemical Fabrication trap; alkali denatures DNA duplexes without transmuting deoxyribonucleotides into ribonucleotides."
