    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 5 (Questions 25&ndash;30)</div>\n<p>Cardiovascular biomarker quantification relies on enzyme-linked immunosorbent assays (ELISA) to detect myocardial injury. Following acute myocardial infarction (AMI), ischemic necrosis of cardiac myocytes causes intracellular proteins to leak across permeabilized sarcolemmal membranes into the coronary circulation. Cardiac troponin I (cTnI, 24 kDa) is the clinical gold standard for AMI diagnosis. Because basal circulating cTnI in healthy individuals is extremely low (&lt; 10 pg/mL) and rises within 3&ndash;6 hours post-ischemia, diagnostic platforms demand exceptional analytical sensitivity and specificity.</p>\n<p>Biomedical engineers compared three ELISA configurations for quantifying plasma cTnI:</p>\n<p>1. <em>Direct ELISA:</em> Patient plasma was incubated directly in polystyrene 96-well microtiter plates, allowing total plasma proteins to adsorb non-specifically via hydrophobic interactions. Bound cTnI was detected using an anti-cTnI primary monoclonal antibody directly conjugated to horseradish peroxidase (HRP).</p>\n<p>2. <em>Indirect ELISA:</em> Patient plasma proteins were immobilized on the polystyrene well surface. An unlabeled mouse anti-cTnI primary antibody was added, followed by an HRP-conjugated goat anti-mouse IgG secondary antibody.</p>\n<p>3. <em>Double-Antibody Sandwich ELISA:</em> Microtiter wells were pre-coated with a high-affinity capture antibody targeting an N-terminal epitope (amino acids 27&ndash;40) of cTnI. Unoccupied plastic binding sites were blocked with 3% bovine serum albumin (BSA) in phosphate-buffered saline containing 0.05% Tween-20 (PBST). Patient plasma was added, allowing selective capture of cTnI. Bound antigen was detected by adding a biotinylated secondary detection antibody specific for a distinct C-terminal epitope (amino acids 87&ndash;99) of cTnI, followed by streptavidin-HRP.</p>\n<p>In all platforms, chromogenic 3,3',5,5'-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) substrate was catalyzed by HRP in the presence of hydrogen peroxide. The reaction was halted with 1 M sulfuric acid, and absorbance was measured at 450 nm ($OD_{450}$) (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Analytical performance and patient cTnI measurements across ELISA platforms</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>ELISA Platform Configuration</th><th>Lower Limit of Detection (pg/mL)</th><th>Background Noise (<em>OD_450</em>)</th><th>Non-specific Interference (%)</th><th>Patient cTnI: 0 h Post-AMI (pg/mL)</th><th>Patient cTnI: 12 h Post-AMI (pg/mL)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Direct ELISA</td><td>450.0 &plusmn; 35.0</td><td>0.18 &plusmn; 0.02</td><td>34.2 &plusmn; 2.8</td><td>Undetectable (&lt; 450)</td><td>1,420 &plusmn; 110</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Indirect ELISA</td><td>120.0 &plusmn; 12.0</td><td>0.26 &plusmn; 0.03</td><td>48.5 &plusmn; 3.6</td><td>Undetectable (&lt; 120)</td><td>1,580 &plusmn; 95</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Sandwich ELISA</td><td>4.2 &plusmn; 0.4</td><td>0.04 &plusmn; 0.01</td><td>2.1 &plusmn; 0.3</td><td>6.8 &plusmn; 0.5 (Normal basal)</td><td>1,840 &plusmn; 80</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Sandwich ELISA (Omit BSA Block)</td><td>28.5 &plusmn; 2.2</td><td>0.72 &plusmn; 0.05</td><td>62.4 &plusmn; 4.5</td><td>420.0 &plusmn; 32.0 (False positive)</td><td>2,150 &plusmn; 120</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
