        "e": "<strong>Role of blocking reagents in eliminating non-specific adsorption background (Topic 642):</strong> Polystyrene microtiter plates are engineered with hydrophobic surfaces designed to bind proteins passively through non-covalent hydrophobic and van der Waals interactions. In a sandwich ELISA, after the capture antibody is immobilized on the well surface, substantial areas of bare, unoccupied polystyrene plastic remain exposed between antibody molecules. The **blocking step** involves incubating the wells with an excess of an inert protein that does not cross-react with the assay antibodies\u2014such as **bovine serum albumin (BSA)** or non-fat dry milk. BSA adsorbs to and coats all remaining naked hydrophobic plastic binding sites. When BSA blocking is omitted, downstream reagents (the biotinylated detection antibody and streptavidin-HRP conjugate) adsorb non-specifically to the bare plastic wells. This immobilizes HRP across the well regardless of whether cTnI antigen is present, causing massive catalytic turnover of TMB substrate and generating false-positive background absorbance ($OD_{450} = 0.72$, falsely indicating 420 pg/mL cTnI at 0 hours).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is biologically nonsensical; BSA is an inert blocking protein, not a physiological inhibitor of cardiac troponin.<br>&bull; C is a Mechanism Inversion trap; unblocked wells increase non-specific binding of reagents to the plate, elevating rather than blocking signal.<br>&bull; D is a Chemical Misattribution trap; sulfuric acid stops the reaction; BSA is not an antioxidant or reducing enzyme."
