        "a": 3,
        "e": "<strong>Spectrophotometric quantification and the Beer-Lambert Law (Topic 642):</strong> In an ELISA assay, the enzymatic reporter (HRP) oxidizes colorless TMB substrate in the presence of $H_2O_2$ to generate a diimine product that turns bright yellow upon acidification with sulfuric acid ($\\lambda_{\\max} = 450\text{ nm}$). Because the amount of bound enzyme is directly proportional to the amount of captured analyte, the concentration of yellow chromophore produced within a fixed incubation time reflects analyte concentration. The optical density (absorbance, $A$) of the chromophore is measured using a spectrophotometer and is governed by the **Beer-Lambert Law**: $A = \u000barepsilon \\cdot c \\cdot l$, where $\u000barepsilon$ is the molar extinction coefficient (absorptivity) of the oxidized TMB chromophore at 450 nm, $c$ is the molar concentration of the chromophore in the well, and $l$ is the optical pathlength of the light beam traversing the liquid column. Thus, within the linear dynamic range, measured absorbance ($OD_{450}$) is directly proportional to chromophore concentration.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes buffer pH equilibria ($pH = pKa + \\log([A^-]/[HA])$), not spectrophotometric absorbance.<br>&bull; B describes enzyme kinetics; while HRP follows Michaelis-Menten kinetics, product absorbance is governed by Beer-Lambert law.<br>&bull; C describes molecular diffusion flux, not optical absorbance."
