        "a": 2,
        "e": "<strong>Signal amplification in Indirect ELISA vs Direct ELISA (Topic 642):</strong> The structural architecture of Indirect ELISA provides intrinsic enzymatic **signal amplification**. In a Direct ELISA, each primary antibody carries an attached enzyme (HRP). Because excessive chemical conjugation can disrupt the antibody's antigen-binding Fab paratope, primary antibodies typically bear only 1 to 2 enzyme molecules. When that primary antibody binds an immobilized antigen molecule, only 1 to 2 enzymes are localized at that site. In contrast, in an Indirect ELISA, an unlabeled primary antibody binds the antigen. Then, a polyclonal enzyme-conjugated secondary antibody (raised against the host species of the primary antibody, e.g., goat anti-mouse IgG) is added. The constant Fc domain of the primary mouse antibody displays multiple distinct antigenic epitopes. Consequently, **multiple secondary antibody molecules can bind simultaneously to a single primary antibody molecule**. This multiplies the number of active HRP enzymes per bound antigen, yielding higher chromogenic turnover, steeper standard curves, and greater signal intensity.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes catalytic antibodies (abzymes); standard primary antibodies do not possess intrinsic peroxidase enzymatic activity.<br>&bull; B describes proteases; secondary antibodies are immunoglobulins, not proteolytic enzymes.<br>&bull; D is an Overstatement trap; direct conjugation can partially reduce affinity, but indirect ELISA's superiority is due to polyclonal secondary antibody signal amplification."
