        "a": 3,
        "e": "<strong>Chronological procedural workflow of Chromatin Immunoprecipitation (ChIP) (Topic 643):</strong> Chromatin Immunoprecipitation (ChIP) maps in vivo physical interactions between specific proteins (transcription factors or modified histones) and genomic DNA sequences. The rigorous experimental workflow proceeds chronologically through five essential phases:<br>1. **In Vivo Formaldehyde Cross-linking:** Living cells are treated with ~1% formaldehyde, which permeates intact membranes to form covalent methylene bridges (cross-links) between amino groups of closely associated protein and DNA molecules, freezing native chromatin architecture.<br>2. **Chromatin Shearing (Sonication):** Nuclei are lysed and chromatin is fragmented\u2014via ultrasonic cavitation or micrococcal nuclease (MNase) digestion\u2014into small soluble fragments typically 200 to 500 base pairs in length.<br>3. **Antibody Immunoprecipitation (Pull-down):** Specific primary antibodies targeting the protein of interest (e.g., anti-H3K27ac or anti-Pol II) are incubated with the sheared chromatin and captured using protein A/G magnetic beads.<br>4. **Cross-link Reversal and Digestion:** Following stringent washing, beads are heated (65&deg;C) with high salt (NaCl) and treated with Proteinase K to hydrolyze formaldehyde cross-links and digest away all cellular proteins.<br>5. **DNA Purification and Quantification:** Pure, de-cross-linked DNA is isolated and amplified by quantitative PCR (ChIP-qPCR) or deep sequencing (ChIP-seq) to quantify genomic locus enrichment.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is a Chronological Inversion trap; sonicating before cross-linking would disrupt fragile non-covalent protein-DNA interactions.<br>&bull; B is a Protocol Destruction trap; digesting with proteinase K before antibody immunoprecipitation would degrade the target protein/histone, destroying antibody epitopes.<br>&bull; C describes cross-linking after antibody addition, which leads to non-specific antibody fixation rather than capturing native chromatin states."
