        "e": "<strong>Biochemical mechanism of Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization (FISH) (Topic 644):</strong> Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization (FISH) visualizes specific DNA sequences directly within cytogenetic preparations of fixed metaphase chromosomes or interphase cell nuclei. Cellular chromosomal DNA exists in a tightly coiled, double-stranded duplex. Fluorescently labeled DNA probes (such as large bacterial artificial chromosomes, BACs, labeled with fluorophores via nick translation) are also double-stranded. For probe hybridization to take place, both the probe DNA and the target chromosomal DNA immobilized on the glass microscope slide must undergo **thermal co-denaturation** (heating to 75&deg;C\u201380&deg;C in the presence of formamide to lower melting temperature). High temperature melts the Watson-Crick hydrogen bonds, separating duplexes into single strands. Upon lowering the temperature (37&deg;C\u201342&deg;C overnight), the labeled single-stranded probe molecules anneal (hybridize) specifically to their complementary target sequences on the chromosomes, guided by canonical base pairing.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes restriction endonuclease digestion, which would fragment chromosomes and wash away target loci from the microscope slide.<br>&bull; C describes reverse transcription, which is used for RNA analysis, not DNA FISH.<br>&bull; D describes UV damage/cross-linking; hybridization relies on reversible Watson-Crick hydrogen bonding, not UV cycloaddition."
