        "e": "<strong>Epigenetic and transcriptional regulation downstream of BCR-ABL kinase inhibition (Topic 643):</strong> In untreated K562 CML cells, hyperactive BCR-ABL1 kinase drives constitutive downstream signaling cascades (JAK2-STAT5, PI3K-AKT, and MAPK/ERK) that maintain active chromatin architecture at oncogenic promoters, notably *MYC*. Histone H3 lysine 27 acetylation (**H3K27ac**) is a classical epigenetic marker of open, active euchromatic enhancers and promoters. In Table 1, untreated K562 cells display high H3K27ac enrichment (8.45% of input) and high **RNA Polymerase II (Pol II)** recruitment (5.20% of input) at the *MYC* promoter, driving proliferation (100%). When treated with imatinib (an ATP-competitive tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting BCR-ABL1), kinase signaling is arrested. Consequently, chromatin-modifying enzymes shift: histone acetyltransferases detach, while histone deacetylases (HDACs) de-acetylate H3K27, causing H3K27ac occupancy to plummet to 1.12% and Pol II occupancy to drop to 0.65%, extinguishing *MYC* transcription and collapsing cell proliferation to 18.4%. In K562-T315I cells, the isoleucine gatekeeper mutation sterically blocks imatinib binding, maintaining high H3K27ac (8.15%) and Pol II (4.98%).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is biologically impossible; imatinib is a small-molecule kinase inhibitor, not a gene-editing enzyme, and does not reverse chromosomal translocations (FISH remains 98.2% 1R1G2F).<br>&bull; B is a Functional Inversion trap; imatinib inhibits tyrosine phosphorylation, and promoters are DNA sequences, not kinase phosphorylation substrates.<br>&bull; D describes covalent drug-protein cross-linking, and T315I mutant cells are resistant to imatinib, not stabilized by it."
