        "e": "<strong>Clinical diagnostic advantages of Interphase FISH vs Metaphase Karyotyping (Topic 644):</strong> Classical cytogenetic analysis (G-banded karyotyping) requires dividing cells arrested in metaphase by mitotic spindle inhibitors such as colcemid. Consequently, karyotyping demands living cells, in vitro cell culture for 24 to 72 hours, and a high mitotic index; in many slow-growing tumors, terminally differentiated cells, or hypocellular bone marrow aspirates, metaphases cannot be obtained. In contrast, **interphase FISH** can be performed directly on **non-dividing (interphase) nuclei** in freshly fixed peripheral blood smears, bone marrow touch preps, or formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded (FFPE) tissue sections without requiring cell culture or mitotic arrest. Furthermore, interphase FISH possesses substantially superior spatial resolution, readily detecting submicroscopic chromosomal deletions and gene rearrangements down to ~100 kb that are completely invisible under the 5\u201310 megabase resolution limit of light-microscopic G-banding.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B describes Sanger or Next-Generation Sequencing; FISH uses large probes (~100\u2013650 kb) that detect gross structural rearrangements, not single-nucleotide mutations.<br>&bull; C describes chromogenic in situ hybridization (CISH) or immunohistochemistry; FISH requires epifluorescence microscopy.<br>&bull; D describes biochemical kinase assays or phospho-flow cytometry, not cytogenetic hybridization."
