    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 7 (Questions 37&ndash;42)</div>\n<p>The insulin-responsive glucose transporter 4 (GLUT4, encoded by <em>Slc2a4</em>) mediates postprandial glucose uptake into peripheral tissues. Upon insulin stimulation, intracellular GLUT4 storage vesicles translocate to the plasma membrane, facilitating glucose diffusion down its concentration gradient. Conventional whole-body homozygous knockout mice (<em>Glut4<sup>&minus;/&minus;</sup></em>) exhibit profound growth retardation, reduced adipose tissue mass, marked cardiac hypertrophy, and premature mortality. This severe systemic pathology precludes evaluating the distinct physiological contributions of skeletal muscle versus white adipose tissue to systemic glycemic regulation.</p>\n<p>To overcome embryonic lethality and decouple tissue-specific metabolic functions, geneticists deployed two targeted knockout methodologies:</p>\n<p>1. <em>Cre-LoxP Conditional Knockouts:</em> Mice harboring 'floxed' <em>Glut4</em> alleles (<em>Glut4<sup>fl/fl</sup></em>), wherein exon 4 is flanked by two 34-base-pair loxP recognition sequences in direct orientation, were crossed with transgenic lines expressing Cre recombinase under tissue-selective promoters:</p>\n<p>&bull; <em>Muscle Creatine Kinase (MCK-Cre):</em> Drives Cre expression selectively in striated skeletal muscle and myocardium.</p>\n<p>&bull; <em>Adiponectin (Adipoq-Cre):</em> Drives Cre expression selectively in mature brown and white adipocytes.</p>\n<p>In Cre-expressing cells, the recombinase recognizes the identical loxP sequences and catalyzes intramolecular recombination, excising the intervening exon 4 as a circular DNA fragment and generating a frameshift null allele. In non-Cre-expressing tissues, the floxed allele functions normally.</p>\n<p>2. <em>Somatic CRISPR/Cas9 Ribonucleoprotein Disruption:</em> Adult wild-type mice received systemic intravenous delivery of adeno-associated virus serotype 9 (AAV9) encoding <em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em> Cas9 and a single guide RNA (sgRNA) targeting exon 4 immediately adjacent to a 5'-NGG protospacer adjacent motif (PAM). Cas9 introduces a targeted double-strand break (DSB) 3 bp upstream of the PAM. Subsequent error-prone Non-Homologous End Joining (NHEJ) generates insertions and deletions (indels), disrupting the reading frame across transduced organs.</p>\n<p>Mice were evaluated at 12 weeks of age for tissue-specific GLUT4 protein expression, fasting blood glucose, and glucose tolerance test (GTT) area under the curve (AUC) (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Tissue-specific GLUT4 protein levels and metabolic glycemic metrics across genetic mouse models</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Genetic Mouse Model</th><th>Skeletal Muscle GLUT4 (% WT)</th><th>Adipose GLUT4 (% WT)</th><th>Myocardium GLUT4 (% WT)</th><th>Fasting Glucose (mg/dL)</th><th>GTT Glucose AUC (mg&middot;h/dL)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Wild-Type Control (*Glut4<sup>fl/fl</sup>*, Cre&minus;)</td><td>100.0 &plusmn; 4.2</td><td>100.0 &plusmn; 5.1</td><td>100.0 &plusmn; 4.8</td><td>82.4 &plusmn; 3.5</td><td>185.0 &plusmn; 12.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Conventional Knockout (*Glut4<sup>&minus;/&minus;</sup>*)</td><td>&lt; 1.0 (Ablated)</td><td>&lt; 1.0 (Ablated)</td><td>&lt; 1.0 (Ablated)</td><td>148.6 &plusmn; 8.2</td><td>480.0 &plusmn; 26.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Muscle-Specific KO (*Glut4<sup>fl/fl</sup>* ; *MCK-Cre*)</td><td>4.2 &plusmn; 0.6</td><td>98.4 &plusmn; 4.8</td><td>5.1 &plusmn; 0.7</td><td>118.2 &plusmn; 5.4</td><td>392.0 &plusmn; 21.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Adipose-Specific KO (*Glut4<sup>fl/fl</sup>* ; *Adipoq-Cre*)</td><td>97.8 &plusmn; 4.5</td><td>3.5 &plusmn; 0.5</td><td>98.6 &plusmn; 4.6</td><td>88.5 &plusmn; 4.1</td><td>224.0 &plusmn; 15.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Systemic AAV9-CRISPR/Cas9 (Exon 4 sgRNA)</td><td>28.4 &plusmn; 2.5</td><td>32.1 &plusmn; 3.0</td><td>26.5 &plusmn; 2.4</td><td>112.4 &plusmn; 6.0</td><td>345.0 &plusmn; 18.0</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
