        "a": 2,
        "e": "<strong>Mechanism of Cre-loxP site-specific recombination (Topic 646):</strong> The Cre-loxP system from bacteriophage P1 is a site-specific tyrosine recombinase tool. A loxP recognition site is an asymmetric 34-base-pair sequence consisting of two 13-bp inverted palindromic repeats flanking an 8-bp asymmetric core spacer sequence that defines the site's directional orientation. The topological outcome of Cre-mediated recombination is dictated strictly by the relative orientation of the two loxP sites:<br>&bull; **Direct Repeats (Same Orientation):** When two loxP sites flank a target DNA sequence in the **same linear direction** ('floxed' gene), four Cre recombinase molecules assemble at the sites, form a synaptic tetramer, and catalyze reciprocal strand cleavage and exchange. This reaction **excises** the intervening DNA sequence as a closed circular molecule, leaving exactly one intact loxP site in the chromosomal locus.<br>&bull; **Inverted Repeats (Opposite Orientation):** If the two loxP sites face toward each other in opposing directions, Cre catalyzes the **inversion** (reversal) of the intervening sequence.<br>&bull; **Translocation:** If loxP sites reside on distinct chromosomes, Cre catalyzes an interchromosomal translocation.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes Cre-mediated inversion, which occurs when loxP sites are placed in opposite (inverted) orientations, not in direct repeat orientation.<br>&bull; B describes unequal crossing-over during meiosis, not site-specific Cre-loxP recombination.<br>&bull; D describes non-homologous end joining (NHEJ); Cre recombinase is a precise, high-fidelity site-specific tyrosine recombinase that does not produce random indels."
