        "a": 0,
        "e": "<strong>Overcoming embryonic lethality and pleiotropy via conditional gene targeting (Topic 646):</strong> In conventional (germline) knockout models, targeted deletion of a gene is present in every cell of the organism from the single-cell zygote stage onward. Many essential metabolic, developmental, and regulatory genes are **pleiotropic** (acting in multiple organ systems) or embryonically indispensable. When such genes are deleted globally, embryos frequently suffer premature developmental death (embryonic lethality) or develop severe multi-organ systemic pathologies (such as the cardiac hypertrophy, growth retardation, and early mortality seen in global *Glut4* null mice). These systemic defects make it impossible to deduce the gene's physiological role in adult tissues. **Conditional gene targeting** via the Cre-loxP system circumvents this limitation by utilizing tissue-specific promoters (such as *MCK* for striated muscle or *Adipoq* for adipocytes) or inducible promoters (e.g., tamoxifen-activated Cre-ERT2). This permits normal embryonic development and restricts gene inactivation exclusively to the anatomical compartment of interest.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B is a Practical Misconception; conditional models require ongoing breeding of floxed and Cre driver transgenic lines.<br>&bull; C describes oncogenic chromosomal rearrangements, whereas Cre-loxP targeting is designed for clean, tissue-specific gene ablation.<br>&bull; D describes prokaryotic defense systems; restriction endonucleases are not active in mammalian embryos."
