        "e": "<strong>Pronuclear microinjection vs targeted homologous recombination (Topic 647):</strong> In classical transgenic mouse generation developed by Gordon and Ruddle, linearized foreign recombinant DNA (containing a promoter and gene of interest) is mechanically microinjected directly into the visible **male pronucleus of a fertilized one-cell zygote** before pronuclear fusion. The injected foreign DNA integrates into the host chromosomal genome via non-homologous, illegitimate recombination mechanisms. Consequently, transgenic integration via pronuclear microinjection has two major characteristics: (1) **random genomic integration**, occurring at unpredictable chromosomal loci that may cause insertional mutagenesis by disrupting endogenous host genes or suffer from positional variegation due to local heterochromatin silencing; and (2) integration of **variable tandem concatemers** (head-to-tail arrays containing anywhere from 1 to over 100 copies). In contrast, CRISPR-directed homologous recombination or embryonic stem cell targeting inserts precisely a single copy at a predetermined, defined genomic locus.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes targeted knock-in via homologous recombination in embryonic stem cells, not pronuclear microinjection.<br>&bull; C describes viral episomes (e.g. Epstein-Barr virus) or yeast artificial chromosomes, not microinjected transgenes, which integrate into host genomic DNA.<br>&bull; D is a Mechanism Fabrication trap; zygotes lack Cre recombinase unless specifically engineered, and pronuclear microinjection relies on host NHEJ machinery for random insertion."
