        "e": "<strong>Ribosomal RNA abundance and enrichment strategies in RNA-seq library construction (Topic 648):</strong> In eukaryotic cells, total cellular RNA is overwhelmingly dominated by structural ribosomal RNA (**rRNA**, specifically 28S, 18S, 5.8S, and 5S rRNA molecules), which accounts for **80% to 90%** of all cellular RNA mass. Transfer RNAs (tRNAs) constitute another 10% to 15%, while protein-coding messenger RNA (mRNA) constitutes only 1% to 5% of the total RNA pool. If total unselected RNA were directly converted into cDNA and sequenced on a high-throughput platform, over 85% of all sequencing reads would map redundantly to ribosomal RNA genes, providing no information regarding gene expression and wasting enormous sequencing capacity and financial cost. To maximize informative sequencing depth across protein-coding transcripts, RNA-seq library preparation protocols strictly mandate either: (1) **poly(A) enrichment**, using magnetic oligo(dT) beads that capture mature eukaryotic mRNAs via their 3' polyadenylated tails; or (2) **rRNA depletion** (e.g., Ribo-Zero), where sequence-specific antisense DNA oligonucleotides hybridize to rRNAs and target them for enzymatic degradation by RNase H.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is a Chemical Misconception trap; reverse transcriptase readily transcribes rRNA into cDNA, which is precisely why it must be removed.<br>&bull; B describes adapter incompatibility; synthetic adapters are ligated to all fragmented cDNAs unless rRNA is removed beforehand.<br>&bull; C describes ribozyme peptidyl transferase activity, which forms peptide bonds during translation, not ribonuclease degradation of mRNA."
