        "e": "<strong>Principles of dual-color competitive cDNA microarray hybridization (Topic 649):</strong> A DNA microarray consists of a solid substrate (glass slide or silicon chip) spotted with thousands of microscopic, sequence-specific, single-stranded oligonucleotide probes corresponding to distinct genes. In a dual-color comparative hybridization assay: (1) mRNA from normal reference tissue is reverse-transcribed into cDNA labeled with green-emitting **Cy3** fluorophore; (2) mRNA from tumor tissue is reverse-transcribed into cDNA labeled with red-emitting **Cy5** fluorophore; and (3) equal masses of Cy3-cDNA and Cy5-cDNA are mixed and allowed to competitively hybridize to the spotted oligonucleotide probes. Following stringent washing, laser confocal microscopy images green and red fluorescence emission:<br>&bull; **Bright Red Spot:** Indicates the gene is upregulated in the tumor (Cy5-cDNA outcompetes Cy3-cDNA).<br>&bull; **Bright Green Spot:** Indicates the gene is downregulated in the tumor (Cy3-cDNA outcompetes Cy5-cDNA).<br>&bull; **Yellow Spot:** Results from equal optical mixing of red and green fluorophores, indicating **equivalent competitive hybridization** and therefore equal expression of that gene in both normal and tumor specimens.<br>&bull; **Black (No Signal):** Indicates the gene is not expressed in either tissue.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes a red spot (Cy5 dominant, reflecting elevated tumor expression).<br>&bull; B describes a black or unspotted locus where neither probe hybridized.<br>&bull; D describes non-specific hybridization background, not equal dual-probe competitive binding."
