        "e": "<strong>Flow cytometry optical physics: Forward Scatter (FSC) vs Side Scatter (SSC) (Topic 650):</strong> In flow cytometry and fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS), suspended single cells are focused into a narrow hydrodynamic stream that traverses an interrogation point illuminated by a laser beam. As each individual cell passes through the laser, it scatters light and excites bound fluorophores. Detectors record two distinct physical light-scattering parameters:<br>1. **Forward Scatter (FSC):** Light scattered at narrow angles (typically 0.5&deg; to 10&deg;) along the direct forward path of the laser beam is collected by a photodiode. Forward scatter is produced primarily by light diffraction around the perimeter of the cell and is **directly proportional to the cell's surface area, cross-sectional diameter, and overall cell size/volume**.<br>2. **Side Scatter (SSC):** Light scattered at orthogonal angles (typically 90&deg;) to the laser beam is collected by a photomultiplier tube. Side scatter is caused by internal reflection and refraction from cellular organelles, granules, and nuclear lobulation, correlating with **internal cytoplasmic granularity and optical complexity** (e.g., distinguishing smooth lymphocytes from highly granular neutrophils).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B describes side scatter (SSC, detected at 90 degrees), which reflects internal complexity, organelle density, and granularity, not cell size.<br>&bull; C describes fluorophore emission, which measures specific antibody-bound antigen abundance, not physical cell volume.<br>&bull; D describes the Coulter principle (impedance aperture), not optical flow cytometric light scattering."
